`checkout` in `commerce/service.ts` and `reserve` in `inventory/service.ts` are the heart of the concurrency lesson. While reading, write down:

- what is done to the cart lines before the fingerprint is computed, and what goes into the advisory-lock key;
- what the lookup by `(tenant, actor, key)` does in each case it handles;
- the exact `WHERE` clause of the reservation `UPDATE`, and what `reserve` does when it returns no row;
- which client every write runs on, and what `transaction()` in `packages/database/src/index.ts` does on an error.

Then follow the payment job in `platform/jobs.ts`, `payments/provider.ts` and `payments/service.ts`. Note which job states and lease conditions `claimJob` selects, how the provider looks up an earlier result, which conditions `applyPaymentResult` checks before writing, and how `retryDelayMs` grows with the attempt number. Look up PostgreSQL's READ COMMITTED behaviour for concurrent updates of the same row.

A request to Forge passes several separate checks, and the order matters as much as the checks. Read `apps/api/src/app.ts` and `apps/api/src/modules/identity/service.ts` and note, for each gate, what it inspects, which error code it throws and with which status:

- the `onRequest` hook near the top of `app.ts`: which methods it applies to and which headers it compares;
- `getSession`: what makes a token unacceptable;
- `authorize`: how it finds the membership for the tenant in the URL, and what it does when there is none;
- `requireWrite` and `requireAdmin`: which roles each refuses.

Then open the checkout route and list what it parses and checks, in the order the code does it. The lesson's Exercise asks what a viewer sees; work it out from that order before you run the curl commands. Note also why the API might prefer one status over another when a user is not a member of a tenant.
